{
    "background_story": "Hezekiah was a 19th-century farmer who lived in rural America. He was known for his innovative approach to animal husbandry, particularly when it came to raising roosters. In an effort to make his flock more docile and increase their value as meat birds, Hezekiah experimented with caponization - a process of neutering roosters to reduce aggression and promote weight gain. Through trial and error, he perfected the technique, creating a thriving business selling caponized roosters to local butchers. His farm became renowned for producing the plumpest, most tender birds in the region, and Hezekiah's expertise in caponization was sought after by farmers from neighboring towns. His innovative approach to animal husbandry not only improved his farm's productivity but also earned him a reputation as a pioneer in the field.",
